    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so it's basically just the sugars and proteins reacting to the heat? is there anything else that affects how much browning happens, like pH levels or anything like that? i want to make sure i get the details right for the exam.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Exactly—it's the amino acids and reducing sugars reacting, but pH is a huge factor. The Maillard reaction proceeds much faster in alkaline conditions (higher pH), which is why adding a pinch of baking soda to cookie dough or using alkaline ingredients like cocoa powder can boost browning. Conversely, acidic ingredients like lemon juice or buttermilk slow it down, which is why pastry doughs with high acid stay paler. Water activity also matters: you need enough moisture for the reactants to move, but not so much that it keeps the temperature below 140°C. Free amino acids and certain sugars (like fructose vs. sucrose) speed things up compared to others, and the presence of metals like iron can also act as a catalyst.",
      "source": "target",
